Hi Alexandre, hi all,

A short progress update since my reply about pveproxy and latency.

The native proposal still uses Alexandre's shared per-VM D-Bus foundation,
with QSM as a separate consumer. The foundation, WebRTC API and PVE UI are
kept as separate series. I have not treated the configuration or service
split as an agreed upstream interface.

To make testing possible without installing rebuilt Proxmox packages, I
have also packaged the standalone profile for official PVE 9, with a
guarded UI overlay and a PVE-authorized HTTPS signalling service. Its
default configurable port starts at 18007, leaving PBS's 8007 alone.
There is now a signed APT repository for these packages:

https://rdmitry0911.github.io/qsm-rd/

An optional, explicitly enabled single-node gateway shares TCP 8006 between
PVE HTTPS, signalling and authenticated TURN/TCP, for a router forwarding
only that port. I checked VM/LXC connections and reconnects in Chrome and
Firefox in a controlled NAT/firewall lab. This is separate from the native
pveproxy/API proposal. TURN control uses plain TCP; HTTPS signalling, SRTP
media and DTLS-protected data remain encrypted. Its listener/SPICE impact
and rollback are documented, and direct UDP remains available.

The media worker is shared by the two profiles. Recent work adds an
optional local cursor with the newer LXC compositor and confirms packet
loss over a rolling window before reducing bitrate, avoiding the reproduced
static-text quality change on isolated loss. Sustained congestion still
adapts. These are functional checks, not a new interaction-latency benchmark.

Source, debs, current installation notes and test evidence:
https://github.com/rdmitry0911/qsm-rd/releases/tag/v0.4.0-qsm-direct-20261020-stock3

For the native work, I would still appreciate feedback on the VM-wide
display option, ownership/lifetime of the bus and the API/service boundary
before submitting an updated numbered series. The LXC desktop provisioning
and standalone gateway remain separate add-ons.

Thanks,
Dmitry
